Make adbd's TCP listener opt-in, and bind it on every interface

The Ubuntu adbd only opens a TCP listener when no USB FunctionFS endpoint is
present, and it binds that listener to loopback (gListenAll defaults to 0). On
the RPC the USB adb function is present, so the daemon would serve USB alone and
never be reachable with `adb connect <ip>:5555` over the network.

Listen on TCP as well and on every interface -- but only when the environment
asks for it (BANANA_ADB_TCP, set by usb-adb from the handheld's SSH setting),
because this adbd has auth_enabled = 0: an open TCP listener is an
unauthenticated root shell, so it should ride the same switch as sshd rather than
be on whenever the device is on a network. USB is always served.

--- a/core/adbd/adb.c
+++ b/core/adbd/adb.c
@@ -1327,7 +1327,10 @@
-    if (!usb) {
-        printf("Using USB\n");
-        // listen on default port
-        local_init(DEFAULT_ADB_LOCAL_TRANSPORT_PORT);
-    }
+    // Banana: also listen on TCP (the stock code only does this when USB is absent), but only when usb-adb
+    // asked for it, and on every interface (gListenAll) so `adb connect <ip>:5555` reaches it over Wi-Fi.
+    // USB is always served; the environment gate keeps an unauthenticated listener off the network by default.
+    if (getenv("BANANA_ADB_TCP")) {
+        gListenAll = 1;
+        local_init(DEFAULT_ADB_LOCAL_TRANSPORT_PORT);
+    }
+    (void)usb;
 
     D("adb_main(): pre init_jdwp()\n");
